# The interactive aerosol in the longwave. DUST-3 item 5.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG. ExoPlaSim's transported aerosol acts in the two SHORTWAVE
# bands and nowhere else: `lwr` had no aerosol term of any kind. A model that
# can cool with dust and cannot warm with it does not have a dust error, it has
# a dust error of one sign, and on this world the shortwave-only figure and the
# true one do not even share a sign. Take the sizes from
# `analysis/dust_forcing.json`; this comment says which way the argument runs.
#
# THE PHYSICS IS NOT NEW AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT RE-DECIDED.
# `exoplasim-3.4.2-prescribed-dust.patch` item 5 already put dust in this
# solver for the PRESCRIBED path: a grey absorber, no longwave scattering,
# at the same 1.66 diffusivity the cloud term uses, multiplied into the total
# layer transmissivity so that overlap with water vapour and CO2 is handled by
# construction rather than by a declared window transmittance. This patch adds
# one branch to that same term. It changes no coefficient and no form.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY ADDS.
#
#  1. `aeroprof`, beside `dustprof` and called from the same place in
#     `radstep`. It builds `daerod`, the band-1 extinction optical depth per
#     layer, from the transported number density: `nrho*PI*apart**2*qex1*dz`,
#     which is the arithmetic `swr` used to do inline. Once per radiation step,
#     because the layer thicknesses follow the temperature profile.
#  2. `swr` now READS that field instead of rebuilding it. One statement of the
#     optical depth, so the shortwave and the longwave cannot drift apart. This
#     is the structure the LMD Generic PCM uses (CeCILL, read for design only,
#     nothing copied): `aerosol_opacity.F90` builds one per-layer optical depth
#     at a reference wavelength and every band, infrared included, is that field
#     times a per-band ratio held with the optics in
#     `rad_correlatedk_ini_aerosol.F90`. Here the ratios are `qex2/qex1` in the
#     shortwave and `aeroqlw` in the thermal infrared.
#  3. `aeroqlw` in `radmod_nl`, the thermal-infrared ABSORPTION optical depth
#     per unit band-1 extinction optical depth, and the `lwr` branch that uses
#     it. It is separate from `dustqlw` because the two paths carry different
#     particles: the prescribed field is the offline chain's size distribution,
#     the tracer is whatever `aero_nl`'s `apart` and `rhop` describe.
#  4. `iaerint`, set once in `radini` from `l_aero` and `l_aerorad`, so
#     `radstep`, `swr` and `lwr` all ask the same question in the same way.
#  5. Two refusals in `radini`. `l_aerorad = 1` with `aeroqlw` at its zero
#     default ABORTS, for the reason `ndustrad = 1` with `dustqlw` at zero
#     already does: a shortwave-only aerosol is a larger error than no aerosol,
#     so there is no defensible default and the run stops rather than pick one.
#     And `l_aerorad = 1` together with `ndustrad = 1` ABORTS, because a
#     prescribed column and a transported one are two aerosols and their optical
#     depths would add.
#
# NO-OP UNTIL A NAMELIST KEY TURNS IT ON. `l_aerorad` defaults to 0 and every
# run this project has made also sets `L_AERO = 0`, so `iaerint` is 0,
# `aeroprof` never runs, `daerod` stays zero and `ztaudu0` is exactly the 1.0 it
# was. One executable therefore runs both arms of the A/B, which is what
# WORKFLOW.md A3 requires and what the low-I/O patch's restart layout makes
# non-negotiable.
#
# PREDICTIONS, with what would falsify each.
#
#  1. NO-OP. On every configuration that exists today, with or without
#     `NDUSTRAD = 1`, a binary with this patch produces output BIT-IDENTICAL to
#     one without it. Falsified by any difference at all.
#  2. IDENTITY, and this is the test with a right answer. Set `aeroqlw` equal to
#     `dustqlw` and give the interactive path a number density whose `daerod`
#     equals the prescribed `ddustod` layer for layer. The longwave fluxes of
#     the two arms must then agree to round-off, because they evaluate the same
#     expression. Falsified by any systematic difference, which localises the
#     error to `aeroprof` rather than to the solver.
#  3. REACHABILITY. Two arms differing only in `aeroqlw`, one at the value the
#     optics give and one an order of magnitude below it, must differ in
#     outgoing longwave radiation. A difference of exactly zero means `lwr` is
#     reading `ddustod` where it should read `daerod`, which is the one
#     substitution that silently produces a correct-looking shortwave-only run.
#  4. SIGN AND SIZE. At the same column optical depth, adding this term returns
#     the several W/m2 of global-mean top-of-atmosphere warming that
#     `analysis/dust_forcing.json` prices between shortwave-only and full dust,
#     to within the difference between a grey absorber and that file's
#     Planck-weighted band integral. Falsified by a term that cools, or by one
#     large enough to make dust net warming here, which would contradict the
#     offline chain at the same burden.
#
# BASE. The stack of exoplasim-3.4.2-aerosol-apart.patch, which has to be
# applied first: this patch edits the `swr` aerosol block that patch's comments
# describe and the `radini` broadcasts it adds to. That base is
#
#   radmod.f90    74a5ad2804ca70b4a0ec985deeb3e442ee950363cec6cf4680f891ba4d4186bf
#
# RESIDENT-DESTINED, NOT YET RESIDENT. Listed in `PENDING_PATCHES` in
# `exoplasim/scripts/rebuild_binaries.py`; it moves to `RESIDENT_PATCHES` in the
# commit that applies it and rebuilds every binary.
#
# IT MOVES THE STAR-CYCLE PIN, together with the apart patch it sits on.
# `build_star_cycle_exoplasim.sh` pins the sha of the radmod.f90 it patches, and
# the star-cycle patch is REGENERATED against the new base rather than
# re-pinned.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not make the interactive path runnable. The
# emission scheme, the three boundary codes, the gathers and the writing of
# `aero_namelist` are DUST-3 item 4 and are not here; with those absent,
# `l_aerorad = 1` gives the radiation an aerosol with no source. This patch is
# the radiation half, and its tests above are all reachable by prescribing
# `nrho` rather than by emitting it.
--- a/radmod.f90
